  SELECT * FROM table2\n)\nSELECT * FROM Intersect\nUNION ALL\nSELECT * FROM table3;\n```\nThis rewritten query takes advantage of the rule\'s transformations to improve performance and maintainability."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 2:\n"""Rule 3 applies to the given SQL query because it contains functions or operations within the SELECT, WHERE, JOIN conditions, or any part of the query that is executed multiple times for the same row.\n\nThe rule moves repeated function calls or operations outside of loops, if applicable. It also replaces inline functions in the SELECT or WHERE clause with a pre-calculated column if the function is deterministic and the input data does not change frequently.\n\nFor example, the original query could be rewritten as follows:\n```sql\nWITH PreCalculated AS (\n  SELECT id, name, expensive_function(column) as expensive_result\n  FROM table\n)\nSELECT * FROM PreCalculated;\n```\nThis rewritten query takes advantage of the rule\'s transformations to improve performance and maintainability."""'}], 'response': 'Here is a summary of the provided SQL query rewrite strategies in one paragraph:\n\nThe given SQL query can be rewritten using two strategies. The first strategy, Rule 2, transforms traditional filtering mechanisms such as NOT EXISTS, NOT IN, EXISTS, IN, OR within JOINs and WHERE clauses into UNION ALL operations to enhance code maintainability and performance. This involves replacing IN with INTERSECT for querying intersecting datasets and using EXCEPT instead of NOT IN or anti-joins to minimize duplicate row processing and optimize resource use. The rewritten query would look like a series of UNION ALL operations. The second strategy, Rule 3, moves repeated function calls or operations outside of loops if applicable and replaces inline functions in the SELECT or WHERE clause with a pre-calculated column if the function is deterministic and the input data does not change frequently. This involves creating a temporary table or view to store the calculated results and then selecting from that table or view.', 'time': 1.884763240814209}
